      "name": "How often should I water mature trees in NE Ohio?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Mature, established trees rarely need supplemental watering in a normal-rainfall year. The exception is drought summers — when 30-day rainfall drops below an inch — and prolonged dry spells. Aim for one inch of water per week during the growing season, applied slowly at the dripline (not the trunk) with a soaker hose left on a slow drip for one to two hours." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "When is the best time to prune trees in Ohio?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Late winter — February through early March — is the best general window for most NE Ohio species. Trees are dormant, structural problems are visible without leaves, and wounds seal cleanly before spring pathogen pressure. The critical exception: no oak pruning April 1 through July 31, when beetles vector oak wilt. Roubic refuses oak work during that window regardless of urgency." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is topping a tree ever appropriate?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No. Topping — cutting the leaders off the top of a tree to reduce height — permanently destroys structure, opens massive decay entry points, and triggers weakly attached water sprouts that fail catastrophically five to ten years later. If a tree is genuinely too tall for its location, the answer is crown reduction by a qualified pruner, not topping. Topping was standard 30 years ago and is now considered malpractice." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I know if my tree is healthy?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Healthy trees show full leaves of normal size and color throughout the canopy, no large dead branches, no fungal conks at the base, no visible trunk decay or cracks, no sudden lean, and steady seasonal growth. Compare year to year: smaller leaves, a sparser canopy, or noticeably more deadwood signals decline. Roubic provides free hazard assessments — we'd rather flag a problem early than respond to a failure." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Should I mulch around my trees?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes, but correctly. A three-foot ring of mulch two to three inches deep conserves soil moisture, moderates soil temperature, and suppresses weeds. The mulch should not be piled against the trunk — that volcano pattern causes bark rot, root girdling, and a shortened tree life. Mulch should look like a donut, not a volcano." }
    }
